# Time Keeper

> A small keeper that counts machine ticks and stores each count as a binary number with bit 1 then bit 0 on the back.

## Purpose
Give the inner-sandbox computer a keeper of ticks. Each tick is one step of the machine clock. The keeper writes the count into a register using the same encoding as every other word.

## How it works (plain English)
The clock starts at zero.
The program takes five ticks.
After each tick the current count sits in r1, already marked with 1 then 0.
When the five ticks are done the keeper copies that count into r2 so it is held, then prints both the live tick and the kept snapshot.
Then it stops.

## Key ideas
- Tick (the clock moves forward by one)
- Keep (hold the current clock without moving it)
- Mark (bit 1 then bit 0 on the back of every number)
- Barrage (this page is for people; only the actual code runs)

## The actual code
```
TICK r1
TICK r1
TICK r1
TICK r1
TICK r1
KEEP r2
PRINT r1
PRINT r2
HALT
```

## Line-by-line translation
1. `TICK r1` → “Move the clock forward by one and put that count in r1.”
2. `TICK r1` → “Tick again. r1 now holds two.”
3. `TICK r1` → “Tick again. r1 now holds three.”
4. `TICK r1` → “Tick again. r1 now holds four.”
5. `TICK r1` → “Tick again. r1 now holds five.”
6. `KEEP r2` → “Copy the clock as it stands into r2. Do not move the clock.”
7. `PRINT r1` → “Show the live tick word.”
8. `PRINT r2` → “Show the kept snapshot word.”
9. `HALT` → “Stop the machine.”

## Decisions the program makes
- Every TICK raises the clock by one and writes the new count, marked 1 then 0.
- KEEP never raises the clock. It only holds what is already there.
- PRINT never changes a register.
- HALT ends the run.

## What a person would notice
After the run, both r1 and r2 hold the same marked word for five: `10110`.
The clock on the machine also stands at five.

## Current status
Runnable on the inner-sandbox computer.

## Next possible steps
- Tick a register-chosen number of times instead of five fixed ticks
- Keep a list of snapshots, not only one
- Print the mark bits separately from the count
